   :adr-covers "Superproject ADR, amended 2026-08-30 (owner instruction): the permanent invariant is no UNTRACKED control effects. Slices 1 and 2 of the typed abort ability landed 2026-09-02 (owner decision: build it, fail-closed, in slices); [:result T E] remains the surface every backend carries, and is what the ability lowers to. E is any value type -- a :document was measured end to end, and lang/compat/clojure/core.kotoba provides ex-info / ex-message / ex-data on it. What is still refused: the export boundary, E disagreement, and the guarded lexical contexts."}
  :bounded-admission
  {:disposition :intentional-security-constraint
   :shielding-axis :resource-bounds
   :limits {:max-functions 1024
            :max-parameters 5
            :max-bindings 4096
            :max-symbol-chars 128
            :max-expression-nodes 50000
            :max-list-items 128
            :max-source-bytes 1048576
            :max-reader-depth 512
            ;; fuel64 (2026-09-03): the EXECUTION budget's ceilings, which this
            ;; entry did not state at all until now. Two numbers rather than
            ;; one, because the two counters have different exact ranges and
            ;; averaging them would make one of the two backends wrong.
            :max-fuel-native 9007199254740991
            ;; A STRING, and the reason is the finding itself: this file is
            ;; read by `clojure.edn` on BOTH runtimes, and on the cljs reader
            ;; 4611686018427387903 comes back as 4611686018427388000. The
            ;; wasm ceiling cannot be stated exactly as a number in a document
            ;; the JVM-free route reads -- which is the same fact, one layer
            ;; up, that puts the native ceiling at 2^53-1.
            :max-fuel-wasm "4611686018427387903"}
   :fuel-ceilings
   {:as-of "2026-09-03"
    :invariant :a-budget-is-exactly-counted-by-every-runtime-that-counts-it
    :native
    {:value 9007199254740991
     :is "2^53-1, Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER"
     :decided-by "kotoba.kir/max-fuel"
     :because "The native charge is `dec qword [r9+8]` (x86-64) / `sub x16,x16,#1` (AArch64), exact to 2^63-1, and the KIR oracle's counter is a Long on the JVM -- but a DOUBLE on Node, and the JVM-free route is the one Q9 makes normative. Measured on Node 2026-09-03: at 9,007,199,254,740,996 (2^53+4) the expression `x - 1 === x` is already true, and from 2^54 up it is true of every value. A budget above 2^53-1 is one the oracle would never see reach zero, so it would answer :ok for a program that does not terminate -- the single answer a fuel bound exists to prevent."
     :enforced-by ["kotoba.kir/execute -- :fuel-outside-admitted-range"
                   "kotoba.verifier -- native fuel budget is not admitted"
                   "kotoba.compiler.nbb.cli/native-fuel! -- Number.isSafeInteger, which was already this bound"
                   "kotoba.native.elf64/replenish-bytes -- :object-fuel-tier-outside-admitted-range"
                   "kotoba.native.interrupt-abi/entry-bytes -- :isr-entry-fuel-exceeds-imm32 (the ISR entry stays imm32 by design; its frame size is load-bearing)"]}
    :wasm
    {:value "4611686018427387903"
     :is "2^62-1, written as a string because the cljs EDN reader rounds it to 4611686018427388000"
     :decided-by "kotoba.wasm/max-fuel"
     :because "A wasm module's counter is an i64 global throughout -- `global.get`, `i64.eqz`, `i64.sub` -- with no double anywhere in the path, so the exact range is wider. Unchanged by fuel64."}
    :not-averaged "Two counters, two exact ranges, two ceilings. A wasm budget of 2^62 handed to the KIR interpreter stalls on its first charge (measured: 4611686018427388000 - 1 === 4611686018427388000), so the ceilings are not interchangeable and neither is a bug in the other."
    :what-changed "Until 2026-09-03 nothing here stated a fuel ceiling, and the number that acted as one was an accident of encoding: `kotoba.native.elf64` wrote an object's per-call budget with `mov qword [r9+8], imm32`, whose sign-extended immediate tops out at 2,147,483,647. The context field is a qword and always was; the image route always wrote eight data bytes. Two ADRs had already reasoned from 2^31-1 as though it were the ABI (aiueos ADR-0142 sized the sha256-region window from it, aiueos ADR-0175 concluded evaluate_token cannot be one object because the output projection alone wants 13x it). Separately, `kotoba.verifier` and the JVM-free CLI admitted at most 2^20 -- four hundred million times tighter than the tiers the object route was actually shipping, and unnoticed because the object route does not read the sealed budget."
    :widening-argument "Fuel bounds execution so a guest cannot spin forever, and a 64-bit budget still bounds it -- what changes is how long `bounded` is. That is a real cost and it is named rather than waved at: a per-call tier is chosen per object by measurement with a stated margin (kotoba.native.elf64's tier table), and a tier near the ceiling on an object the kernel calls with interrupts disabled is a hang, not a bound. The ceiling is what the mechanism may carry; it is not a recommendation, and no shipped object is near it."
    :adr ["docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-fuel-budget-is-sixty-four-bits.md"
          "kotoba-native docs/adr/0078"
          "kotoba-kir docs/adr/0268"
          "aiueos 90-docs/adr/0195"]}
   :reason "Untrusted and generated programs must fail closed under finite admission and execution resources."
   :adr ["docs/adr/ADR-reliability-t54-max-parameters.md"
         "docs/adr/ADR-reliability-t72-fuel-model.md"
         "docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-fuel-budget-is-sixty-four-bits.md"]
   :adr-covers "t54 decides :max-parameters 5; t72 decides the fuel model behind execution bounds; fuel64 decides the two ceilings and the invariant that sets them."
   :adr-gap "No ADR decides :max-functions, :max-bindings, :max-expression-nodes, :max-list-items, :max-symbol-chars, :max-source-bytes or :max-reader-depth. Those seven limits are enforced but undecided on paper (measured 2026-08-12). The two fuel ceilings left that list on 2026-09-03."}
