      :fused-read-modify-write
      {:landed "kotoba-sema 8b2cb101 (2026-09-01), fused-rmw? in kotoba.compiler.affine. Admits (vector-assoc! v k (+ (vector-at v k) delta)) -- read and write at the SAME index in one compound expression -- as ONE use rather than two, by requiring the read to be the update expression's only mention of the handle and the index to be the identical source form on both sides. Kotoba expressions are pure, so a form written twice is a value computed twice, not two different reads."
       :why-it-was-needed "Before this there was no expression at all for v[k] += delta. vector-assoc! and vector-at are two calls, counted as two uses regardless of what either did, so torihiki.book's slab/add! (book.cljc:521, :538) had no Kotoba equivalent. This was the second of the three walls recorded under :arena-bounds."
       :evidence-end-to-end "kotoba.sema/analyze on that shape: before the commit it throws the used-more-than-once refusal; after, it admits and returns valid HIR. Six sub-cases pin both directions -- two admits and four refusals that must NOT move: mismatched index, an extra mention of the handle in the update, a read after the fused write consumed it, and two fused writes on one path."
       :evidence-native-lowering "amu #735 (merged 0bf63c94), native_executor_test.clj/fused-read-modify-write-is-what-lets-a-slab-be-written-more-than-the-arena-allows. Same differential as the constant-write case, over the fused shape: 300 fused increments of a 256-slot vector finish under vector-assoc! and trap under vector-assoc at the copying update's 255-write arena budget, with the trapped run holding fuel REMAINING and stopping EARLIER than the run that completed. That last pair is what makes it evidence rather than a green light -- it rules out the loop simply being too long. So the admitted program is not merely admitted, it lowers to a real store on the real kexe_loader process."
       :not-directly-measured "wasm32-browser and js-kotoba-v1 were not re-run for this SHAPE. Backend lowering dispatches on the vector-assoc! / vector-at heads, not on what a source expression nested inside them looks like, so the same lowering should apply -- but that is an inference from :backend-lowering above, not a repeated measurement."}
      :was-a-gate-that-could-not-go-green
      "Until 2026-09-01 linear? could not admit ANY let-bound handle. sym-uses
       counted the let BINDER as a use, and position-ok? read the same bare
       name in the binding vector as an escape -- two independent reasons.
       Found by compiling a correctly threaded program: amu check refused it
       AND the double-write program with the same message, so the gate said
       nothing in either direction. The 16 tests passed throughout, because
       they exercised the two predicates nothing calls. Fixed in kotoba-sema
       9f016ce; the four new tests pin linear? on the live path."
      :corrected "The first version (kotoba-native 79c383fb) admitted a let that rebinds one name repeatedly, which amu refuses with duplicate let binding -- a gate nothing could ever pass -- and summed the arms of an if, which refused the shape Kotoba actually uses: a vector threaded through a tail-recursive parameter, read in the base case and consumed in the step. Both found by compiling a program rather than reasoning about one (2026-09-01, amu e96dd8c6). Fixed in kotoba-native d6ee1387."}
     :source-head-and-gate
     {:status :met
      :evidence "vector-assoc! is admitted by kotoba-sema: arity, a type signature identical to vector-assoc, and check-affine-writes! refusing a handle that is not linear over the whole function body. Measured end to end 2026-09-01: a tail-recursive writer is admitted, and one that reads the handle beside the write is refused by name -- vector-assoc! requires a linear handle: v is used more than once on some path. kotoba-kir admits the op and its interpreter treats it as the SAME operation as vector-assoc, so the reference semantics cannot drift from the lowering."}
