   :limits [:element-type-f32-declared-not-admitted
            :not-in-a-record-set-option-result-or-vector
            :not-returned :not-across-an-export-boundary]
   :f32-note "`[:slice :f32]` is DECLARED and refused. It is the element type the carrier is ultimately for -- the Qwen weights are binary32 -- and there is no `slice-load-f32` on either native ISA. Recording it as available before the load exists would be amu ADR 0284's defect in miniature."
   :decided-by "amu ADR 0285; carried value kotoba-sema ADR 0022"
   :authority-gap
   "CLOSED 2026-09-03 by ADR-the-authority-names-every-head-the-frontend-admits.
    `lang/guest-grammar.edn`'s `:admitted-builtins` named three kernel
    operations while the frontend admitted a hundred and fourteen. It now
    names all hundred and fourteen -- fifty-three memory, eight carried
    slice, fifty-three privileged -- and a test in this repository and in each
    of the three vendoring repositories compares the copies rather than
    assuming they agree.

    Three of the four byte copies were resynced. kotoba's two were NOT: its
    five classpath copies all agree with each other at the previous
    authority, and its own check (which is stricter than this repository's --
    every copy byte-identical, no exemption) would refuse a half-resync,
    while a full one means advancing an amu pin a hundred and six commits
    behind. That gap is recorded there as a baseline naming both digests
    rather than closed or left silent.

    What that resync also uncovered: the four byte copies had ALREADY
    diverged. `local-and-sibling-vendors-match-authority` tolerates an absent
    sibling, and a single-repository clone has no siblings at all, so the
    check measured nothing and reported green -- amu's copy was one change
    behind (local-state slice 1) and kotoba's two copies were two hundred
    lines behind, on main, unreported. The replacement check lives where the
    copy is on a CLASSPATH, so it cannot be absent, and it prints how many
    copies it compared.

    STILL OPEN: the fifty-three privileged operations are now NAMED in the
    grammar but have no `:checked-memory`-shaped family here, so they fall to
    `:default-for-missing` (`:not-yet-implemented`) while shipping on both
    native ISAs. That is the same understatement this entry was opened for,
    one file over. It is recorded rather than invented: writing a status entry
