  {:disposition :implemented-partial
   :current "The recorded bounded language slices now run on compiler and primary Wasm/CLJS; representation and performance contracts remain deliberately narrower than Clojure."
   ;; What the cited evidence is, measured 2026-08-12 by reading it.
   ;;
   ;; `:primary-test` is not a parity test. Its suite deftest is named
   ;; `authority-positive-cases-run-on-primary-wasm`: it compiles each case,
   ;; runs it through `wasm-exec/run-main` on ONE backend, and compares the
   ;; result to the `(:kotoba expect)` literal recorded in the manifest. It
   ;; never runs a case on a second backend, so it cannot witness parity
   ;; between two.
   ;;
   ;; Corrected 2026-08-12, same day: the first version of this audit also said
   ;; a case that fails to compile asserts nothing, reading the
   ;; `(when (:kotoba.wasm/ok? wasm) ...)` guard in `run-case` as the whole
   ;; story. It is not — `compile-source` and `compile-run-case` each carry
   ;; their own `(is (:kotoba.wasm/ok? wasm) ...)`, so a compile failure does
   ;; fail the suite. The guard only suppresses a second, cascading failure on
   ;; the result comparison.
   ;;
   ;; What IS uncovered is narrower and countable. Measured against the
   ;; authority manifest: 37 cases, 34 of kind :run/:compile-expr, 32 carrying
   ;; a `:kotoba` expect, and 31 reaching a result assertion.
   ;;
   ;; Corrected a second time, 2026-08-12. The first list of uncovered cases
   ;; named `:entry-extension-clj` and `:entry-extension-cljs` as "missing a
   ;; :kotoba expect", which read as a defect in the cases. It is not. Those
   ;; two declare `:required-backends #{:host-clj}` and `#{:host-cljs}` and
   ;; carry `{:clj 25}` / `{:cljs 45}` — the whole point of the
   ;; `entry_extensions` family is that the extension selects the HOST, and a
   ;; `.clj` entry is not supposed to have a Kotoba result. Adding a `:kotoba`
   ;; expect there would assert something the case was written to deny.
   ;;
   ;; The gap is a missing runner, not a missing expect. `:host-clj` and
   ;; `:host-cljs` are declared backends in the manifest
   ;; (`:status :host-compat`), 7 of the 37 cases require one of them, and no
   ;; runner in kotoba, kotoba-lang or the compiler executes either — searched
   ;; src and test in all three, zero hits. So 13 recorded `:clj`/`:cljs`
   ;; expect values are never compared against anything, and the two cases that
   ;; require ONLY a host backend are executed by nothing at all.
   ;;
   ;; This is the mechanism behind `:cljs-parity-coverage :none` below: the
   ;; manifest declares a CLJS host, cases carry `:cljs` expects, and nothing
